From 866d71838ae436b44c52a01da89ef98849b64035 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Uwe Augustin <Info@FlapsOnline.de>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 17:49:42 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 15/16] OpenVarioSystemWidget: back up and restore the whole
 system

The Data Management dialog backs up the data directory of XCSoar, but
an OpenVario holds more that a pilot wants to keep when the SD card is
replaced: the SSH keys, the WiFi networks of ConnMan, the boot
settings, the touch screen calibration and the state of the services.
The image has transfer-system.sh for that, which copies all of it,
together with the home directory, to the USB stick and back.  XCSoar
runs it in a process dialog.

The profile is saved before a backup, so that the backup holds the
settings the pilot sees.  A restore overwrites the profile on disk,
and XCSoar must not write its own copy over it when it quits; the
profile is therefore marked unmodified and the device restarts right
away, which the restored system settings need anyway.

transfer-system.sh does not check for the USB stick itself.  Without
one, it depends on the automount unit whether rsync fails or copies
the backup into the mount point directory on the root file system,
filling it up, and a restore would read from there.  XCSoar therefore
looks for a stick first: the image mounts it through a systemd
automount unit, which leaves an "autofs" entry in /proc/mounts even
without a stick, so only a mount of another type on /usb/usbstick
counts.  That decision is a pure function and is covered by the test.
